1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,133
An AI proposal is easy to write.

2
00:00:03,133 --> 00:00:06,648
The harder question is whether it becomes accountable work.

3
00:00:06,648 --> 00:00:11,724
This demonstration follows one recorded survey order
through A.R.C., a mission-planning simulator using

4
00:00:11,724 --> 00:00:13,677
Mars as its first environment.

5
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:21,335
This is read-only inspection of an accelerated software
study, not a new mission

6
00:00:21,335 --> 00:00:24,987
running now. The survey target is 175.15 metres.

7
00:00:24,987 --> 00:00:28,182
Its source describes a published rover record.

8
00:00:28,182 --> 00:00:33,660
The application preserves that citation and explicitly
says: record, not typical performance.

9
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:40,170
Here, Muse Wonder requested an additional survey job using
the create-job tool.

10
00:00:40,170 --> 00:00:45,337
The stored receipt contains the model, public tool
arguments and provider response identifier.

11
00:00:45,337 --> 00:00:50,504
This is the director's concrete output, rather than a
paragraph claiming that work

12
00:00:50,504 --> 00:00:50,901
happened.

13
00:00:55,400 --> 00:01:00,834
Muse Spark then submitted the work order, naming the job,
rover and evidence

14
00:01:00,834 --> 00:01:06,268
references. A separate assurance request approved it while
acknowledging missing site obstacle analysis.

15
00:01:06,268 --> 00:01:11,285
That condition remains visible. Approval here means
permission inside this configured simulator.

16
00:01:11,285 --> 00:01:16,719
It does not establish safe physical navigation, and the
complete rover energy model

17
00:01:16,719 --> 00:01:17,555
remains unvalidated.

18
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:29,931
The work-order record separates the proposal from the
simulator result.

19
00:01:29,931 --> 00:01:35,821
It records accepted validation, the input snapshot,
calculation and completed outcome: 175.15 metres

20
00:01:35,821 --> 00:01:38,992
at tick 1976. These are simulated metres.

21
00:01:38,992 --> 00:01:44,882
Zero grid power means no draw from the outpost grid; it
does not

22
00:01:44,882 --> 00:01:47,600
mean the rover uses no energy.

23
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:53,291
Each accepted decision retains the source versions it
cited.

24
00:01:53,291 --> 00:01:57,466
I can move from the job into its input, calculation and
result, then

25
00:01:57,466 --> 00:02:01,641
into the decision trail. That lets a reviewer examine the
basis of the

26
00:02:01,641 --> 00:02:04,532
action instead of relying on the agent's summary alone.

27
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:20,339
This saved verification report records authenticated
retrieval of 89 provider responses from the

28
00:02:20,339 --> 00:02:25,278
study. For the survey submission, it records matching
response identity, model, token usage

29
00:02:25,278 --> 00:02:29,457
and public tool calls. This is evidence from the recorded
run.

30
00:02:29,457 --> 00:02:34,396
It is not a fresh API call or independent access to an
account's

31
00:02:34,396 --> 00:02:35,156
billing dashboard.

32
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:45,141
The demonstrated result is a traceable software workflow: a
cited input, a Meta-generated

33
00:02:45,141 --> 00:02:48,423
work order, explicit authorization and a recorded simulator
outcome.

34
00:02:48,423 --> 00:02:51,341
It gives a reviewer something concrete to inspect.

35
00:02:51,341 --> 00:02:56,082
It does not establish that the site is safe or that an
autonomous

36
00:02:56,082 --> 00:02:57,541
colony is physically feasible.
